      "title": "Mindfulness, attention, and mechanism-level caveats",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Subsystem-specific attention effects",
          "body": "Mindfulness training evidence points to changes in specific attentional subsystems rather than a single global attention benefit. The attention-training source reports improved orienting after an 8-week MBSR course, altered alerting after a 1-month intensive retreat among experienced meditators, and baseline conflict-monitoring differences in participants with prior concentrative practice."
        },
        {
          "title": "Alpha suppression as a comparison mechanism",
          "body": "Alpha-band attention research supplies a mechanism-level comparison point. Across visual, auditory, tactile, spatial, feature-based, and intersensory contexts, alpha activity is described as increasing in cortical regions that process irrelevant or distracting information, consistent with active sensory suppression rather than a generic attention improvement."
        },
        {
          "title": "Limits on broad mindfulness claims",
          "body": "The synthesis remains cautious because mindfulness constructs and interventions are heterogeneous, the attention-training study used small groups without active-training controls and behavioral measures only, and broad mindfulness claims can exceed the specificity of available behavioral and neural evidence."
        }
