      "title": "Operationalizing mental constructs across consciousness, attention, and mindfulness research",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Shared measurement problem",
          "body": "The sources share a concern with turning broad mental concepts into measurable structures. Integrated information theory defines consciousness through complexes and phi, attention studies separate alerting, orienting, conflict monitoring, and sensory suppression, while mindfulness research warns against treating mindfulness as a unitary or universally defined construct."
        },
        {
          "title": "What instruments capture",
          "body": "Measurement choices shape interpretation. Self-report mindfulness scales vary in facets and factor structures, ANT scores depend on predefined cue and target contrasts, alpha studies depend on paradigms that isolate suppression rather than orienting, and integrated information theory depends on identifying informational relationships within a complex."
        },
        {
          "title": "Construct-validity focus",
          "body": "A useful synthesis compares how different fields define the target construct, what each measure captures, and where construct validity remains uncertain. This avoids merging consciousness, attention, and mindfulness claims into one generic account of mental states."
        }
