        {
          "title": "Uneven clinical and public evidence",
          "body": "The mindfulness critique argues that clinical, media, educational, corporate, and military enthusiasm has outpaced an uneven evidence base. Preliminary clinical trials, condition-specific benefits, under-assessed adverse effects, opportunity costs, and cautious interpretation of neuroimaging all limit broad implementation claims."
        },
        {
          "title": "Small-scale attention-study caution",
          "body": "The attention-training source reinforces the need for caution at a smaller scale. Its reported behavioral attention effects are preliminary because of small groups, lack of active-training controls, behavioral-only measurement, protocol differences, and possible task-repetition effects."
        },
        {
          "title": "Reform agenda",
          "body": "The resulting cross-source topic is not that mindfulness is ineffective, but that claims about benefits, mechanisms, and safety require precise intervention descriptions, active controls, adverse-event monitoring, preregistration, replication, and modest communication of neuroimaging or behavioral results."
        }
